From 4ba088b742fd1dbab81073a122c19c7d46164350 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:57:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban: missing-parent repair card may unblock its
 own parent_missing child

The dispatcher blocks a child as parent_missing and files a repair card that "owns investigation and
recovery", and the repair brief says to unblock the child. But no path let that card do it: the
kanban_unblock tool had no grant for it, and the operator-call home check (_agent_block) accepted only
a block whose actor is the target's own agent run. On 11-12 the repair worker for t_01a1cfbe6c8d46e7
fell back to `hermes kanban unblock` over ssh, so the unblocked event read actor `user` (David).

One grant, two entry paths:
- kanban_requeue.missing_parent_repair_grant: a card created_by='missing-parent-detector' may unblock
  exactly its creator child, only while the child is blocked with no human request and its newest
  blocked event is source=parent_missing naming this repair card as repair_task_id.
- kanban_unblock tool (same gateway): requeue_grant returns via=missing-parent-repair; the grant is
  re-checked inside the unblock txn; the unblocked event carries actor `agent (task <repair>)` and
  repair_task_id. When the child exists here only as a Board replica copy, the tool forwards the
  unblock as an operator call instead of failing.
- operator call home (_agent_block): keyed on the Board-proven caller.task_id, same grant, also in
  the unblock_task precondition; the unblocked event keeps operator_request_uid / operator_caller.
- Repair brief: unblock with kanban_unblock (works across gateways); never the hermes CLI over ssh.

Hand, Desktop and policy holds, other workers, other targets, a parent_missing block superseded by a
later hand block, and one naming a different repair card all stay refused (one test each, both paths).
Card t_01a1d3a8ebbe939d.
